# diegops Rust CLI rules

## Project context

`diegops` is a personal productivity CLI (Rust stable + clap v4) that must run identically on:
- Windows (WSL / native): `x86_64-pc-windows-msvc`
- macOS Intel/ARM: `x86_64-apple-darwin`, `aarch64-apple-darwin`
- Ubuntu/Debian: `x86_64-unknown-linux-gnu`
- Alpine (containers): `x86_64-unknown-linux-musl`, `aarch64-unknown-linux-musl`

Both interactive human use and headless container/CI use are first-class targets.

## Cross-platform rules

- Paths: `PathBuf::from` / `.join()` — never `format!("{}/{}", ...)` for paths
- No shell assumptions: don't invoke `sh`, `bash`, `cmd.exe`, `powershell` without OS detection
- Platform-specific code: wrap in `#[cfg(target_os = "...")]` with explanatory comment
- musl/static: shared code must not introduce dynamic glibc dependencies

## Code quality — non-negotiable

```rust
// All code must compile clean with:
// cargo fmt --check
// cargo clippy -- -D warnings
```

- No `.unwrap()` in production paths — use `?`
- `main()` returns `Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>>` and propagates with `?`
- No unused imports or dead code
- Public items require `///` doc comments
- Error propagation via `Result<T, E>` and `?`

## Exit codes

- `0` — success (idempotent no-ops also exit 0)
- `1` — user/input error
- `2` — internal/unexpected error

Never use `std::process::exit()` outside `main`. Let `main() -> Result` handle exit codes via the `?` operator.

## stdout vs stderr

- `println!` → data / machine-readable output (stdout)
- `eprintln!` → errors, warnings, progress (stderr)

This separation is mandatory for pipeline use: `diegops cmd | jq` and CI log collection depend on it.

## TTY / container compatibility

The binary runs without a TTY in Docker containers and CI environments:
- No interactive prompts — all inputs via flags/arguments
- Custom ANSI/color: guard with `std::io::IsTerminal` before emitting escape codes
- `clap` automatically respects `NO_COLOR`; don't override it
- Never assume stdin is attached to a terminal

## Idempotency

Commands must be safe to run N times. Prefer upsert semantics. Never error on already-existing state.

```rust
// Wrong: errors on second run
fs::create_dir(&path)?;
// Right: idempotent
fs::create_dir_all(&path)?;
```

## Testing rules

- Unit tests: `#[cfg(test)]` inline
- Integration tests: `tests/` directory using `env!("CARGO_BIN_EXE_diegops")`
- Zero network calls in tests
- All tests must pass on all 6 targets
- Always assert `output.status.success()` or check the specific exit code

## Commands

| Command | Description |
|---------|-------------|
| `diegops version` | Print version string |
| `diegops update` | Self-update from latest GitHub release |
| `diegops help` | Show full help |

`diegops update` implementation notes:
- Target triple detected at **compile time** via `#[cfg]` constants — never at runtime
- HTTP: `ureq` with `default-features = false, features = ["tls", "json"]` (rustls, no OpenSSL)
- Archive: `flate2` + `tar` on Unix; `zip` on Windows (platform-specific Cargo deps)
- Replace: atomic `fs::rename` on Unix; rename-to-`.old` + rename-new on Windows
- Network is needed at runtime — integration tests only call `update --help`

## Dependencies

For any new HTTP dependency use `ureq` (sync, rustls) — not `reqwest`, not `native-tls`, not `openssl`.
Reason: musl static builds require pure-Rust TLS; OpenSSL is not statically available in all cross-compilation environments.

## Structure

```
src/main.rs              # Thin dispatcher: parse → dispatch → return Result
src/commands/mod.rs      # pub mod declarations
src/commands/<name>.rs   # Non-trivial command logic (extract when >~20 lines)
tests/                   # Integration tests only
```

## Releases

Tag `vX.Y.Z` → GH Actions builds 6 binaries → GitHub Release assets.
Never manually edit workflow version strings; the tag drives everything.

## Runner

All `runs-on` values must be `cadi-hq-runner-dind-set` (self-hosted Linux dind).
Never use `ubuntu-latest`, `macos-latest`, or `windows-latest`.
All release targets use `cross` — the runner is Linux with Docker available.
